it's very important to repair bare spots  in the lawn before weeds have a chance  to invade the process is simple the  first thing you need to do is prepare  the soil get rid of existing vegetation  if there are any weeds there and the  deeper you can work the soil the easier  it's going to be for the grass roots to  penetrate and it'll help help the plants  withstand all kinds of stresses like  like drought and heat so you're going to  want to fill in with some kind of a soil  I've just chosen a top soil that I  picked up at the garden center and I've  also included some compost compost is a  good way to help improve the physical  properties of the soil can help retain  moisture and nutrients so if you've got  a compost pile at home that you want to  use that's a that's a good way to amend  the soil the seed I've chosen is  perennial ryegrass we're in full Sun and  it also has a characteristic of quick  germination which is something that  you're looking for when you're trying to  fill in a bare area so for a small area  like this I am just going to mix the  grass seed right with the soil and I'm  using a three-to-one ratio of the soil  mix to seed one two three and then one  more of the seed  you wanna firm the soil because when you  have good feed to soil contact it allows  the seed to extract moisture from the  soil and simply by firming the area like  this you can reduce the establishment  time by by up to a couple of weeks so  it's really important and then the final  step is to keep the area watered you  don't want it sopping wet just keep it  moist  you don't want the seed or the seedlings  to dry out once they've dried out  they're done so once you've got good  germination you can back off on the  water that that will encourage the roots  to go deeper
 

